1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:02,988
Where does a payment system start, if not with payments?

2
00:00:02,988 --> 00:00:07,690
It starts with a project that builds, and an environment that runs with one command.

3
00:00:07,690 --> 00:00:15,166
The system uses Java twenty-five and Spring Boot four, with Gradle builds written in Groovy, the way the bank writes its own.

4
00:00:15,166 --> 00:00:25,630
The app has six modules, partner, commission, terminal, BIN, card, and payment, and each one owns its own schema, with a database user that can see nothing else.

5
00:00:25,630 --> 00:00:32,988
A module talks to another only through the public interface in its root package, and the build fails if it reaches any further.

6
00:00:32,988 --> 00:00:39,607
Shared pieces live in a platform folder, each its own build, published to the local Maven repository.

7
00:00:39,607 --> 00:00:45,083
The version catalog pins every version, so the build is the same on every machine.

8
00:00:45,083 --> 00:00:56,559
Errors follow one format: each module lists its errors in an enum, and a YAML file gives each one its HTTP status and its message in English, Russian and Uzbek.

9
00:00:56,559 --> 00:01:05,261
API contracts are OpenAPI files, and the code generated from them lives only in the published jars, never in our source.

10
00:01:05,261 --> 00:01:13,714
Every module follows the same layers, controller, service, repository, and entity, with its constants in one place.

11
00:01:13,714 --> 00:01:22,416
Services that do one job live outside the app, each with its own contract and client: the card scheme adapters, and the file service.

12
00:01:22,416 --> 00:01:31,785
A separate infrastructure repository uses Docker Compose to start Postgres and Redis, and every other tool joins only when a problem needs it.

13
00:01:31,785 --> 00:01:39,559
With one setting, the app logs structured JSON in the community ECS format, and reports its health from the very first minute.

14
00:01:39,559 --> 00:01:44,071
One command in the infrastructure repository, and the whole world is up.

15
00:01:44,071 --> 00:01:49,726
The code, the infrastructure, the load tests, and the web app each live in their own repository.

16
00:01:49,726 --> 00:01:53,119
Next, the domain, which comes before a single table.

